        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: direct owner",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ਲੋਕਾਂ ਨਾਲ (lokaan naal) — lokā̃ nāl — with people."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The standalone form is ਲੋਕ. Before a postposition, this noun becomes ਲੋਕਾਂ (lokaan). Keep the step narrow: this lesson follows one noun and does not say that every Punjabi noun changes in the same way."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: source boundary",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Johal explains the oblique case before a postposition and shows that this class of masculine plural adds the nasal ending -ਾਂ. Here that source rule is applied only to ਲੋਕ. Source: Ranbir Johal, Speaking and Writing Punjabi (2018), pp. 76–78, oblique nouns before postpositions."
            }
          ]
        },
